    "comments": [
      {
        "id": "comment-1",
        "text": "ENGINE TIMERS\n\nTimer Set starts an engine timer (seconds, looping or not) and returns a handle. When it elapses the\nengine sends a TimerFired event to this object; the 'On TimerFired' event node handles it.\nTimer Clear stops a timer by its handle. Unlike Delay, a timer does not pause a chain, so you can\nrun many at once and cancel them. This example needs the running game (it talks to the event hub).",
        "position": [
          60.0,
          -300.0
        ],
        "size": [
          900.0,
          210.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.2,
          0.4,
          0.8,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-2",
        "text": "START IT\nA looping 1-second timer, started when the object begins play. Its handle is kept in a variable so Timer Clear could stop it later.",
        "position": [
          60.0,
          -70.0
        ],
        "size": [
          840.0,
          100.0
        ],
        "color": [
          0.2,
          0.65,
          0.35,
          0.3
        ],
        "contained_node_ids": [
          "bp",
          "tset",
          "sh"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "comment-3",
        "text": "HANDLE IT\nOn TimerFired runs each time a timer of this object elapses (scope 'self'). Count the firing and print.",
        "position": [
          60.0,
          440.0
        ],
        "size": [
          840.0,
          100.0
        ],
        "color": [
